// @ens-test
// @exit 0
// @stdout PASS metadata answers a file's own length and nothing beside it
// @stdout PASS metadata answers an empty file as empty rather than as unreadable
// @stdout PASS metadata answers a directory with no length of its own
// @stdout PASS metadata answers a modification time inside a plausible window
// @stdout PASS a modification time moves when the content changes
// @stdout PASS a link answers for itself and for what it points to
// @stdout PASS metadata says why it could not answer
// @stdout PASS a path leading through a file says so in each platform's own terms
// @stdout PASS the real path resolves the dots a path is written with
// @stdout PASS the real path says how much room it needs when it is given none
// @stdout PASS the real path says why there is none
// @stdout PASS the current directory is a directory this program can read
// @stdout PASS a number no file call reports classifies as Other
// @stdout 13/13 tests passed
// The bridges std.fs will read a path through, exercised on the host running the suite. A structure
// field read at a wrong offset is what these tests exist to catch, since two of the three seed
// hosts cannot be run by hand.

main() -> int {
    return 0;
}
